The agentskills.codes catalog indexes 11,524 skills as of November 25, 2026, and 6,343 of them sit in the Coding category. Most "best Claude Code skills" lists sort that pile by GitHub stars. That tells you which repo is famous, not what the skill will make your agent do with a dirty working tree and a merge conflict.

So we went the other way. We picked 15 developer-workflow skills from the catalog, pulled each one's SKILL.md from its GitHub source, read it end to end, and checked that the install commands resolve. **Webapp Testing** from Anthropic is the best all-rounder because it ships working scripts and a clear procedure. **Git Commit** is the safest git skill we read. **Resolve Conflicts** is the only one of the 15 that makes the agent write a risk-rated plan and wait for your approval before touching a conflict. The rest are ranked below, with their catches.

{% youtube id="fOxC44g8vig" title="Claude Agent Skills Explained (Anthropic)" /%}

*Anthropic's own explainer video on Agent Skills, the format every pick below uses.*

## Short answer: which skills to install first

- **Best overall:** [Webapp Testing](https://agentskills.codes/skills/webapp-testing). Highest install count of the 15 picks (353) and the only one in the testing group that bundles a runnable server-lifecycle script.
- **Best for git hygiene:** [Git Commit](https://agentskills.codes/skills/git-commit). Reads your repo's commit rules first, does not push, does not skip hooks unless you say so, and its folder was fixed as recently as November 5, 2026.
- **Best for merge pain:** [Resolve Conflicts](https://agentskills.codes/skills/resolve-conflicts). Plan first, approval second, edits third.
- **Best for code review:** [Code Reviewer](https://agentskills.codes/skills/code-reviewer) if you want a short, cheap checklist; [Code Review Excellence](https://agentskills.codes/skills/code-review-excellence) if you want review process and tone coaching.
- **Best for unit tests:** [Vitest](https://agentskills.codes/skills/vitest) for TypeScript, [Python Testing Patterns](https://agentskills.codes/skills/python-testing-patterns) for pytest.
- **Best open source:** all 15 live in public GitHub repos; eight of the nine distinct repos report MIT or Apache-2.0 through the GitHub API (`anthropics/skills` reports none; its folder ships a LICENSE.txt).
- **Best for setting up Claude Code itself:** [Claude Automation Recommender](https://agentskills.codes/skills/claude-automation-recommender), which is read-only and changes no files.
- **Weakest pick, included for honesty:** [Docs Architect](https://agentskills.codes/skills/docs-architect). It is generic, has 2 installs, and is last on the list for that reason.

The catalog's top Coding skill had 1,555 installs on the day we checked, so treat install counts here as a weak tiebreaker, not proof of quality.

## How we ranked them

Three things decided the order, in this priority:

1. **What the SKILL.md actually makes the agent do.** A skill with a concrete procedure, stop conditions and safety rules beats a skill that restates general advice the model already knows.
2. **Freshness of the skill folder.** We asked GitHub for the last commit that touched each skill's own folder, not the repo's last push. Repos like `anthropics/skills` get pushed constantly while a given skill folder sits unchanged for months.
3. **Catalog installs and views,** as a tiebreaker only.

We left out framework-specific skills (the Flutter and Godot skills top the Coding install chart but are not general developer workflow). For every pick we also compared the catalog's copy of SKILL.md with the file on GitHub: after normalizing whitespace, the body text of the hosted SKILL.md is identical to GitHub's for all 15. The hosted copy only rewrites the frontmatter quoting and adds a `source:` line.

