## How Claude Code finds and loads a skill

You need three facts about the host before the picks make sense. They come from the Claude Code skills documentation, fetched November 25, 2026.

First, a skill is a folder with a `SKILL.md`. The folder name becomes the slash command, so `~/.claude/skills/git-commit/SKILL.md` is typed as `/git-commit`. Personal skills live in `~/.claude/skills/`, project skills in `.claude/skills/` (commit them so the team gets them), and when two skills share a name, enterprise beats personal and personal beats project.

Second, the body loads only when the skill is invoked. In a regular session, the docs say, skill descriptions are loaded into context so Claude knows what is available, but the full skill content only loads when invoked. After auto-compaction, Claude Code re-attaches the most recent invocation of each skill and keeps the first 5,000 tokens of each, within a combined budget of 25,000 tokens. The docs advise putting the most important instructions near the top of SKILL.md for that reason. Our picks range from 3,183 to 17,965 characters of SKILL.md, so the short ones are cheap and the heavy ones (Resolve Conflicts, Refactor) are worth installing only in the projects that need them.

Third, Claude Code already ships bundled skills. The docs name `/code-review`, `/debug`, `/batch`, `/doctor`, `/loop` and `/claude-api` among them. That overlaps with our review and debugging picks. We did not run the bundled skills, so we do not claim either is better.

{% figure src="https://claude.com/resources/articles/skills/opengraph-image/og" alt="Open Graph card for Anthropic's Introducing Agent Skills article" caption="Anthropic's Agent Skills announcement card. Source: claude.com" /%}

Skills are also an open format now. The Claude Code docs say skills follow the Agent Skills open standard, and Codex, Gemini CLI, Cursor, OpenCode and Hermes each document the same folder-with-SKILL.md layout. A per-client works-with matrix is further down.

{% x id="1978855432123723909" /%}

*Anthropic's launch post for Skills (October 16, 2025).*

## The 15 picks

Each section below states what the SKILL.md instructs, based on the file we read from GitHub on November 25, 2026. "Last folder commit" is the date of the most recent commit that touched that skill's folder.

### 1. Webapp Testing

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026/logos/webapp-testing.png" name="Webapp Testing" size=40 /%}

**Source:** [anthropics/skills](https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/main/skills/webapp-testing), 200,317 stars on GitHub as of November 25, 2026. Catalog: 353 installs, 585 views. Last folder commit: April 20, 2026. Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/webapp-testing](https://agentskills.codes/skills/webapp-testing).

**What it instructs.** The SKILL.md tells the agent to test local web apps by writing native Python Playwright scripts, and it opens with an unusual rule: always run the bundled helper with `--help` first and do not read its source unless a custom solution is truly needed, because the scripts are large and would pollute the context window. A small decision tree follows. Static HTML: read the file, find selectors, script it. Dynamic app with no server running: use `scripts/with_server.py` to start one or more servers (it supports a backend plus a frontend at once) and run your automation against them. Server already up: do reconnaissance first (navigate, wait for `networkidle`, screenshot or dump the DOM), pick selectors from the rendered state, then act. The one "common pitfall" it spells out is inspecting the DOM before `networkidle` on a JavaScript-rendered page.

**Who it is for.** Frontend and full-stack developers who want the agent to verify UI behavior, capture screenshots and read browser console logs without wiring up a test framework first.

**Pros**
- Ships real tooling: `with_server.py` plus three examples (`element_discovery.py`, `static_html_automation.py`, `console_logging.py`).
- The "black box the script" rule protects your context budget.

**Cons**
- Python-only Playwright. A TypeScript Playwright suite is not what it produces.
- It is a reconnaissance and automation helper, not a test-suite generator; it does not set up assertions, fixtures or CI.
- The hosted zip contains only SKILL.md (we unzipped it, 1 file), so the scripts and examples are missing. Use the `degit` install to get all 6 files.

**Benefits for you.** You get a repeatable way to say "start the dev server, open the page, tell me what is broken" instead of hand-writing Playwright boilerplate each time.

**Pricing and limits.** Free. The folder includes a LICENSE.txt (the GitHub API reports no repo-level license). No usage limits beyond your model tokens.

**Skip it if** your project already has a Playwright or Cypress suite you want extended in place; use the project's own runner instead.

```bash
npx degit anthropics/skills/skills/webapp-testing ~/.claude/skills/webapp-testing
```

### 2. Git Commit

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026/logos/git-commit.png" name="Git Commit" size=40 /%}

**Source:** [github/awesome-copilot](https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/main/skills/git-commit), 41,555 stars. Catalog: 11 installs, 49 views. Last folder commit: November 5, 2026 ("check operation state and extra edits before staging"). Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/git-commit](https://agentskills.codes/skills/git-commit).

**What it instructs.** Six steps: read the repo's rules first (commitlint config, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `git log --oneline -10`, and if a repo rule differs from the skill, the repo wins); examine `git status --porcelain` and the staged and unstaged diffs; stage files; write a Conventional Commits message with type, scope and one-line summary; commit; then verify with `git log -1 --stat` and `git status --porcelain`. Step 3 is where this skill earns its place. It checks for `MERGE_HEAD`, `CHERRY_PICK_HEAD` and `REVERT_HEAD`, lists unmerged files, and if a merge, cherry-pick or revert is in progress it refuses to split the commit or unstage anything, because doing so would silently drop the other side's changes. Otherwise it puts one logical change in each commit and refuses `git add -p` and `git add -i` because those wait for human input.

The safety rules are blunt: do not change git config, do not use `--no-verify` unless told to, if a hook fails fix the problem and make a new commit rather than `--amend`, do not rewrite history, do not push, never force-push the default branch.

**Who it is for.** Anyone who has watched an agent run `git add -A && git commit -m "update"` on a tree full of unrelated edits.

**Pros**
- Strongest guardrails of any git skill we read; it is explicit about secrets (`.env`, `credentials.json`, private keys).
- Respects repository conventions over its own.
- Recently maintained, with a fix landing 20 days before our check.

**Cons**
- 8,575 characters for a task most people consider simple; most of the length is merge-state handling you may never hit.
- Low install count (11), so fewer eyes on it than the stars of its host repo suggest.
- Does not help if you only want a message drafted. The description says so: use it only when you want an actual commit.

**Benefits for you.** Clean, atomic commits that match your repo's conventions, and no surprise pushes.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, MIT (per GitHub API). Needs Git; Git 2.56+ for the `--resolved` path.

**Skip it if** you commit by hand and only want message suggestions.

```bash
curl -L https://agentskills.codes/api/skills/download/546 -o skill.zip && unzip skill.zip -d ~/.claude/skills/git-commit
```

### 3. Resolve Conflicts

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026/logos/resolve-conflicts.png" name="Resolve Conflicts" size=40 /%}

**Source:** [antinomyhq/forge](https://github.com/antinomyhq/forge/tree/main/.forge/skills/resolve-conflicts), 7,639 stars. Catalog: 81 installs, 334 views. Last folder commit: November 27, 2025 ("add plan-first workflow to conflict resolver"). Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/resolve-conflicts](https://agentskills.codes/skills/resolve-conflicts).

**What it instructs.** The description is forceful: invoke this skill immediately when the user mentions merge conflicts, and do not try to resolve them directly. Eight core principles: plan before executing and get approval, prefer keeping both changes, merge rather than choose (especially imports, tests and config), regenerate generated files instead of hand-merging them, back up deleted-but-modified files, run tests after, explain each resolution in one line, and ask when unclear.

The workflow is the differentiator. Step 1 categorizes every conflicted file (regular, deleted-modified, generated, test, import/config, binary). Step 2 produces a written "Merge Resolution Plan" with a conflict type, strategy, rationale and Low/Medium/High risk per file, an execution order and a list of questions, then it waits for your approval. Only then does it run `scripts/handle-deleted-modified.sh` for backups and analysis, and resolve files in plan order. For lock files it says to regenerate with the package manager rather than merge text.

**Who it is for.** Teams with long-lived branches, monorepos and lockfile conflicts, where an agent confidently picking "ours" is expensive.

**Pros**
- Approval gate before any edit, with a risk rating you can scan.
- Bundles `validate-conflicts.sh`, `handle-deleted-modified.sh`, a patterns file and a sample plan (5 files via `degit`).
- The only skill in this list that treats deleted-versus-modified conflicts as their own case.

**Cons**
- 17,965 characters, the heaviest SKILL.md in the list, ahead of Refactor's 16,843. Install it per project, not globally.
- The helper-script paths are written as `.forge/skills/resolve-conflicts/scripts/...`. Installed under `~/.claude/skills/`, those literal paths do not exist, so edit them or run the scripts by their real path.

**Benefits for you.** Fewer silent bad merges, and a reviewable record of why each file was resolved the way it was.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, Apache-2.0 (per GitHub API).

**Skip it if** your conflicts are almost always in one or two files you can read in seconds.

```bash
npx degit antinomyhq/forge/.forge/skills/resolve-conflicts .claude/skills/resolve-conflicts
```

### 4. Code Reviewer

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026/logos/code-reviewer.png" name="Code Reviewer" size=40 /%}

**Source:** [google-gemini/gemini-cli](https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/tree/main/.gemini/skills/code-reviewer), 109,099 stars. Catalog: 24 installs, 62 views. Last folder commit: January 21, 2026. Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/code-reviewer](https://agentskills.codes/skills/code-reviewer).

**What it instructs.** Five short steps. Decide the target: a remote PR (number or URL) or local staged and unstaged changes. For a remote PR, `gh pr checkout <PR_NUMBER>`, run the project's verification suite, and read the PR description and existing comments. For local changes, `git status`, `git diff` and `git diff --staged`, and ask before running preflight if the change is large. Analyze against seven pillars: correctness, maintainability, readability, efficiency, security, edge cases and error handling, and testability. Output a Summary, Findings grouped as Critical, Improvements and Nitpicks, and a Conclusion of Approved or Request Changes. It finishes by asking whether to switch back to the default branch.

**Who it is for.** Developers who want a fast second pair of eyes on a PR or on their own diff, without a long process document in context.

**Pros**
- 3,183 characters. Cheap to keep installed.
- Severity buckets make the output easy to act on.

**Cons**
- The check step is literally `npm run preflight`, a script from the Gemini CLI repo. In your project that script probably does not exist, so tell the agent to use your own test and lint commands.
- No guidance on language-specific pitfalls. For that, see pick 9.

**Benefits for you.** A consistent review format you can paste into a PR comment.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, Apache-2.0. Needs the GitHub CLI (`gh`) for remote PRs.

**Skip it if** you already rely on Claude Code's bundled `/code-review` (named in the Claude Code docs) and are happy with it. We did not run either, so we make no quality claim between them.

```bash
curl -L https://agentskills.codes/api/skills/download/527 -o skill.zip && unzip skill.zip -d ~/.claude/skills/code-reviewer
```

### 5. Vitest

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026/logos/vitest.png" name="Vitest" size=40 /%}

**Source:** [antfu/skills](https://github.com/antfu/skills/tree/main/skills/vitest), 6,099 stars. Catalog: 41 installs, 183 views. Last folder commit: October 23, 2026 ("document expect.unreachable, typed vi.mock, and sync to v5.1.0-beta.1"). Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/vitest](https://agentskills.codes/skills/vitest).

**What it instructs.** This is a documentation-index skill, not a process skill. The SKILL.md opens with a short description of Vitest (Vite-powered, Jest-compatible API, native ESM and TypeScript, V8 or Istanbul coverage), then three tables that route the agent to 19 reference files: Core (config, CLI, test API, describe, expect, hooks), Features (mocking, snapshots, coverage, test context and fixtures, concurrency, filtering, tags, reporters, benchmarking) and Advanced (vi utilities, environments, type testing, projects). The frontmatter records `version: "2026.10.23"` and says the content is generated from the Vitest repo and based on Vitest 5.1.0-beta.1. A `GENERATION.md` in the folder documents how it is regenerated.

**Who it is for.** TypeScript and Vite developers who want the agent to use current Vitest APIs rather than remembered Jest ones.

**Pros**
- Version-stamped and regenerated from upstream docs; the freshest testing skill we read.
- Progressive disclosure done well: the index is 3,716 characters and the details load on demand.

**Cons**
- Pinned to a beta (5.1.0-beta.1). If your project is on an older stable Vitest, some APIs may not match; verify against your installed version.
- The hosted zip holds only the 3.7 KB index, so the references the index points to are missing. Install with `degit` to get all 21 files.

**Benefits for you.** Correct mocking, fixtures and config syntax for the Vitest you actually run.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, MIT.

**Skip it if** you use Jest, Node's built-in test runner or pytest.

```bash
npx degit antfu/skills/skills/vitest ~/.claude/skills/vitest
```

### 6. Python Testing Patterns

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026/logos/python-testing-patterns.png" name="Python Testing Patterns" size=40 /%}

**Source:** [wshobson/agents](https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/main/plugins/python-development/skills/python-testing-patterns), 42,880 stars. Catalog: 77 installs, 204 views. Last folder commit: October 30, 2026 ("correct 22 broken code examples reported in #790"). Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/python-testing-patterns](https://agentskills.codes/skills/python-testing-patterns).

**What it instructs.** A compact pytest primer with a pointer to deeper material. After a use-when list it covers four core concepts: test types, the Arrange-Act-Assert structure, meaningful coverage ("not just high percentages") and test isolation. It recommends a `tests/` layout with `conftest.py`, `test_unit/`, `test_integration/` and `test_e2e/`, a `test_<unit>_<scenario>_<expected>` naming pattern, retry-behavior tests, freezegun for time, markers (`pytest -m slow`, `-m "not slow"`) and `pytest-cov` commands including `--cov-fail-under=80`. Async testing, monkeypatching, property-based and database testing live in `references/advanced-patterns.md` and `references/details.md`.

**Who it is for.** Python developers who want the agent to write pytest the way a seasoned reviewer would expect, not a pile of `assert x == y` scripts.

**Pros**
- The recent history is the best signal here: a commit fixing 22 broken code examples means someone is actually testing the snippets.
- Layered: the main file is 7,282 characters and the long material is on demand.

**Cons**
- Pytest only. No unittest or nose guidance.
- The hosted zip does not include the two reference files that carry the async, monkeypatch and database material, so use `degit` for the full skill.
- Generic advice in places ("Aim for meaningful coverage") that the model already knows.

**Benefits for you.** More consistent test layout and naming across a Python codebase.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, MIT.

**Skip it if** you do not write Python, or your team already has a strong pytest conventions doc in `CLAUDE.md`.

```bash
npx degit wshobson/agents/plugins/python-development/skills/python-testing-patterns ~/.claude/skills/python-testing-patterns
```

### 7. Software Architecture

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026/logos/software-architecture.png" name="Software Architecture" size=40 /%}

**Source:** [davila7/claude-code-templates](https://github.com/davila7/claude-code-templates/tree/main/cli-tool/components/skills/development/software-architecture), 34,192 stars. Catalog: 333 installs, 868 views, the second highest install count among the 15 picks. Last folder commit: January 25, 2026 ("Migrate 244 skills from antigravity-awesome-skills repository"). Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/software-architecture](https://agentskills.codes/skills/software-architecture).

**What it instructs.** Its description says to use it whenever the task relates to software development, which means it can load almost always. The body is a rule list based on Clean Architecture and Domain-Driven Design. Code style: early returns over nested conditions, avoid duplication, decompose functions over 80 lines, split files over 200 lines, prefer arrow functions. Library first: always search for an existing solution before writing custom code (it names `cockatiel` for retry logic), and lists when custom code is justified. Naming: avoid `utils`, `helpers`, `common` and `shared`; use domain names like `OrderCalculator`. It lists anti-patterns (NIH syndrome, business logic in UI components, queries in controllers) and quality limits: max three levels of nesting, functions under 50 lines, files under 200 lines.

**Who it is for.** Teams who want an opinionated house style applied by default, especially JavaScript and TypeScript shops.

**Pros**
- The naming rule is concrete and checkable.
- "Search for libraries first" counters the agent's habit of writing a custom helper for everything.

**Cons**
- It states two different function limits (decompose over 80 lines, but also keep functions under 50 when possible), so the agent has to pick one.
- The auto-trigger description is so broad that it loads on nearly every coding task, spending context for opinions you may not share.
- The rules are JS and TS flavored (arrow functions, npm, Redux, Zustand).
- Last folder commit is from a bulk migration, not a targeted update.

**Benefits for you.** A consistent shape for new code without writing your own style guide.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, MIT.

**Skip it if** you have a strong existing style guide, or you work in a language where these rules do not map.

```bash
curl -L https://agentskills.codes/api/skills/download/658 -o skill.zip && unzip skill.zip -d ~/.claude/skills/software-architecture
```

### 8. Debugging Strategies

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026/logos/debugging-strategies.png" name="Debugging Strategies" size=40 /%}

**Source:** [wshobson/agents](https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/main/plugins/developer-essentials/skills/debugging-strategies), 42,880 stars. Catalog: 3 installs, 7 views. Last folder commit: October 15, 2026. Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/debugging-strategies](https://agentskills.codes/skills/debugging-strategies).

**What it instructs.** Debugging as the scientific method: observe, hypothesize, experiment, analyze, repeat. A four-phase process follows. Reproduce (confirm you can, build a minimal reproduction, document the steps). Gather information (error messages, environment, recent changes, scope). Form a hypothesis (what changed, what is different, where could it fail). Test and verify (binary search, add logging, isolate components, compare working against broken). It then gives patterns by issue type (intermittent bugs, performance problems, production bugs), a "common mistakes" list and a "when stuck" checklist. Tool-specific detail is in `references/tools-and-techniques.md`.

**Who it is for.** Anyone whose agent jumps to a fix before it has reproduced the bug.

**Pros**
- Enforces reproduce-first and one-change-at-a-time, the two habits that most reduce flailing.
- Production-bug guidance is sensible: gather evidence, reproduce locally with anonymized data, and do not change production.
- References are loaded only when bisecting or profiling.

**Cons**
- Only 3 installs. Nobody has told us it works.
- Much of it is a general checklist. Claude Code ships a bundled `/debug` skill (named in the docs); we did not run it, so we cannot say how the two compare.

**Benefits for you.** A repeatable debugging loop and a written record of hypotheses.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, MIT.

**Skip it if** you already have a debugging workflow in your `CLAUDE.md`, or the bundled `/debug` does what you need.

```bash
npx degit wshobson/agents/plugins/developer-essentials/skills/debugging-strategies ~/.claude/skills/debugging-strategies
```

### 9. Code Review Excellence

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026/logos/code-review-excellence.png" name="Code Review Excellence" size=40 /%}

**Source:** [wshobson/agents](https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/main/plugins/developer-essentials/skills/code-review-excellence), 42,880 stars. Catalog: 19 installs, 58 views. Last folder commit: October 15, 2026 ("split 8 oversized skills and correct their content"). Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/code-review-excellence](https://agentskills.codes/skills/code-review-excellence).

**What it instructs.** Where Code Reviewer is a checklist, this is a review philosophy. It covers goals, rules for good feedback and what to leave to tools, then a four-phase time-boxed process: context gathering (2-3 minutes: read the description and linked issue, check PR size and ask to split if over 400 lines, check CI), high-level review (5-10 minutes: architecture, file organization, testing strategy), line-by-line (10-20 minutes: logic, security, performance, maintainability) and a summary decision of approve, comment or request changes. It adds a modified sandwich method for difficult feedback, a five-step approach to disagreements and best practices: review within 24 hours, keep PRs to 200-400 lines, review in 60-minute blocks. Two reference files carry checklists and language-specific patterns, and the SKILL.md says to read them before writing feedback.

**Who it is for.** Team leads and senior engineers who want review comments with good tone and a consistent bar, or who use the agent to draft review comments on others' PRs.

**Pros**
- Addresses the human side: disagreements, difficult feedback, reviewer fatigue.
- Reference files keep language-specific detail out of the default context.

**Cons**
- Written largely for a human reviewer ("take breaks", "60 minutes max"). Some advice does not apply to an agent.
- 6,766 characters plus references: heavier than Code Reviewer for a similar job.

**Benefits for you.** Review comments that explain why and sort by severity, which helps junior authors.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, MIT.

**Skip it if** you want a short checklist. Pick Code Reviewer instead.

```bash
npx degit wshobson/agents/plugins/developer-essentials/skills/code-review-excellence ~/.claude/skills/code-review-excellence
```

### 10. Claude Automation Recommender

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026/logos/claude-automation-recommender.png" name="Claude Automation Recommender" size=40 /%}

**Source:** [anthropics/claude-plugins-official](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-plugins-official/tree/main/plugins/claude-code-setup/skills/claude-automation-recommender), 48,176 stars. Catalog: 47 installs, 140 views. Last folder commit: May 28, 2026 ("add Convex to database/backend MCP coverage"). Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/claude-automation-recommender](https://agentskills.codes/skills/claude-automation-recommender).

**What it instructs.** It is explicitly read-only: it analyzes a codebase and outputs recommendations, and it does not create or modify files. The frontmatter limits it to `Read, Glob, Grep, Bash`. Phase 1 inspects the project (type, dependencies, existing Claude Code config, structure). Phase 2 generates recommendations for five automation types: MCP servers, skills, hooks, subagents and plugins. It recommends one or two of each type by default, three to five if you ask for a specific type, and tells the agent to go beyond its reference lists with web search. Phase 3 prints a report with a codebase profile and a section per type. Five reference files hold the patterns.

**Who it is for.** Developers setting up Claude Code on a repo for the first time, or auditing an old setup.

**Pros**
- Read-only, so safe to run on any repo.
- Output is capped (one or two per category), which avoids a 40-item wishlist.

**Cons**
- It recommends; it does not install. You still do the work.
- Claude Code specific. The hooks and subagent advice does not carry to other clients.
- Folder last changed May 28, 2026, so newer Claude Code features may be absent from its reference lists. The skill tells the agent to search the web for those.

**Benefits for you.** A tailored shortlist of hooks, MCP servers and skills for your actual stack, instead of a generic awesome-list.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, Apache-2.0.

**Skip it if** your Claude Code setup is already tuned, or you use another client.

