### 11. Dependency Upgrade

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026/logos/dependency-upgrade.png" name="Dependency Upgrade" size=40 /%}

**Source:** [wshobson/agents](https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/main/plugins/framework-migration/skills/dependency-upgrade), 42,880 stars. Catalog: 26 installs, 240 views. Last folder commit: March 7, 2026 ("remove phantom resource references and fix CoC links"). Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/dependency-upgrade](https://agentskills.codes/skills/dependency-upgrade).

**What it instructs.** A major-version upgrade playbook, mostly in shell snippets. It starts with a semver refresher, then audits (`npm outdated`, `npm audit`, `npm ls`, `yarn why`), and shows a compatibility-matrix pattern for peer dependencies. The core is a three-phase staged strategy: plan (list current versions, read `CHANGELOG.md` and `MIGRATION.md`, write an `UPGRADE_PLAN.md` with an order), incremental updates ("Don't upgrade everything at once", one major version at a time with test and build in between), then validation. It also covers codemods (`jscodeshift`), testing around the upgrade, Renovate and Dependabot configuration, and a rollback script.

**Who it is for.** Anyone staring at a React, TypeScript or Node major-version bump with 40 outdated packages.

**Pros**
- The "one major at a time, test between" rule is the part that actually saves projects.
- A recent commit removed references to resources that did not exist, which is the right kind of cleanup.

**Cons**
- Examples are npm and JavaScript first. Pip, Cargo, Go modules and Maven get nothing comparable.
- Example versions (React 17, React Router 6) are dated, so the agent has to be told to check current versions.
- Single file with no scripts; it is advice with snippets, not tooling.

**Benefits for you.** A defensible upgrade order and a rollback path written before you start.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, MIT.

**Skip it if** you only need a patch-level bump, or your stack is not JavaScript.

```bash
curl -L https://agentskills.codes/api/skills/download/351 -o skill.zip && unzip skill.zip -d ~/.claude/skills/dependency-upgrade
```

### 12. Git Advanced Workflows

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026/logos/git-advanced-workflows.png" name="Git Advanced Workflows" size=40 /%}

**Source:** [wshobson/agents](https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/main/plugins/developer-essentials/skills/git-advanced-workflows), 42,880 stars. Catalog: 11 installs, 97 views. Last folder commit: October 15, 2026 ("fix the reflog expiry comment and note the deploy script"). Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/git-advanced-workflows](https://agentskills.codes/skills/git-advanced-workflows).

**What it instructs.** A reference card for five techniques: interactive rebase (`pick`, `reword`, `edit`, `squash`, `fixup`, `drop`), cherry-picking, `git bisect` including automated bisect with a test script, worktrees, and the reflog. Then seven best practices that are the useful part: always use `--force-with-lease` instead of `--force`, rebase only local commits, keep commits atomic, test before a force push, remember reflog entries expire (unreachable commits after 30 days by default), and create a backup branch before any risky rebase. A recovery section lists the abort commands and warns that `git reset --hard` deletes uncommitted changes that the reflog cannot recover. Deeper worked examples live in `references/details.md`.

**Who it is for.** Developers who ask the agent to clean up a branch before a PR, or to recover from a mistake.

**Pros**
- The safety habits (`--force-with-lease`, backup branch, check `git status` before `reset --hard`) are exactly what you want an agent to do with history-rewriting commands.
- The reflog-expiry note was corrected in October 2026, which shows the maintainers read their own git facts.

**Cons**
- It does not prevent anything. Unlike Git Commit, it has no hard "never do X" rules, so an agent can still follow the instructions to rewrite shared history if you ask.

**Benefits for you.** Safer history rewrites and a recovery checklist when something goes wrong.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, MIT.

**Skip it if** you only commit and push to a trunk and never rewrite history.

```bash
npx degit wshobson/agents/plugins/developer-essentials/skills/git-advanced-workflows ~/.claude/skills/git-advanced-workflows
```

### 13. Refactor


**Source:** [github/awesome-copilot](https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/main/skills/refactor), 41,555 stars. Catalog: 8 installs, 38 views. Last folder commit: February 2, 2026 (a Codespell spelling fix, so the content itself is older). Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/refactor](https://agentskills.codes/skills/refactor).

**What it instructs.** The SKILL.md opens with five "Golden Rules": behavior is preserved, take small steps and test after each, commit before and after each safe state, tests are essential ("Without tests, you're not refactoring, you're editing"), and do one thing at a time without mixing refactors and features. It then lists when not to refactor (working code that will not change, production code without tests, tight deadlines, "just because"). Ten code smells follow with bad and good examples (long method, feature envy, primitive obsession, magic numbers and others). The rest covers Extract Method, type safety, design patterns, a five-step safe process (prepare, identify, refactor in small steps, verify, clean up) and a quality checklist (functions under 50 lines, no `any` without justification, no circular dependencies).

**Who it is for.** Teams that want refactors to stay behavior-preserving and reviewable, rather than a sprawling rewrite the agent calls "cleanup".

**Pros**
- The rule "add tests first" is the single most useful constraint you can put on an agent that is about to restructure code.
- The "when NOT to refactor" list stops needless churn.

**Cons**
- 16,843 characters in one file with no references folder. At that size, it competes with your actual code for context. Load it for the refactoring task, not permanently.
- Most of the value is in the prose rules and before/after snippets, so it does not know your language's refactoring tools.
- The last meaningful content change is unclear: the latest folder commit is a spelling fix.

**Benefits for you.** Smaller diffs, tests before edits, and a refusal to refactor code with no safety net.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, MIT (the frontmatter also declares `license: MIT`).

**Skip it if** you already enforce small-PR and test-first rules in your `CLAUDE.md`.

```bash
curl -L https://agentskills.codes/api/skills/download/515 -o skill.zip && unzip skill.zip -d ~/.claude/skills/refactor
```

### 14. Subagent Driven Development


**Source:** [davila7/claude-code-templates](https://github.com/davila7/claude-code-templates/tree/main/cli-tool/components/skills/development/subagent-driven-development), 34,192 stars. Catalog: 14 installs, 93 views. Last folder commit: December 20, 2025 ("Add 56 new skills from community repos and custom creations"). Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/subagent-driven-development](https://agentskills.codes/skills/subagent-driven-development).

**What it instructs.** It runs an implementation plan by dispatching a fresh subagent per task, with a two-stage review after each. The controller reads the plan once, extracts every task with its full text and builds a TodoWrite list. For each task: dispatch an implementer subagent (using the bundled `implementer-prompt.md`), answer its questions, let it implement, test, commit and self-review, then dispatch a spec-compliance reviewer (`spec-reviewer-prompt.md`), and only after that passes a code-quality reviewer (`code-quality-reviewer-prompt.md`). Fixes go back to the same implementer, then the reviewer checks again. The red-flags section is strict: never skip a review, never run multiple implementers in parallel, never start the quality review before spec compliance passes, never accept "close enough".

**Who it is for.** Developers who already have a written plan and want to run it with less context pollution and a built-in review loop.

**Pros**
- Review order is explicit: spec first, then quality. This is the main thing that separates it from a plain "use subagents" prompt.
- Ships three prompt templates, so the dispatch prompts are not improvised.

**Cons**
- It depends on other skills. The Integration section names `superpowers:writing-plans`, `superpowers:requesting-code-review`, `superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch` and `superpowers:test-driven-development`. We did not find those in the folder, so on its own the workflow is incomplete.
- Heavy by design: an implementer plus two reviewers per task. Not worth it for small changes.
- Uses Claude Code concepts (TodoWrite, subagent dispatch). Other clients will need adaptation.
- The hosted zip has only SKILL.md; the three prompt templates require `degit`.

**Benefits for you.** Long plans executed task by task with a verification gate on each, instead of one giant session that forgets the spec.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, MIT. Real cost is tokens: three agent runs per task.

**Skip it if** you do not write plans first, or your tasks are small enough for one session.

```bash
npx degit davila7/claude-code-templates/cli-tool/components/skills/development/subagent-driven-development ~/.claude/skills/subagent-driven-development
```

### 15. Docs Architect


**Source:** [sickn33/antigravity-awesome-skills](https://github.com/sickn33/antigravity-awesome-skills/tree/main/skills/docs-architect), 50,306 stars. Catalog: 2 installs, 10 views, category Writing in the catalog. Last folder commit: October 23, 2026 ("integrate reviewed catalog maintenance batches"). Catalog page: [agentskills.codes/skills/docs-architect](https://agentskills.codes/skills/docs-architect).

**What it instructs.** The first half is boilerplate: "Use this skill when" and "Instructions" sections that restate the skill's name ("Working on docs architect tasks"). The real content follows: act as a technical documentation architect, work in three phases (discovery of structure, dependencies and patterns; structuring a chapter hierarchy; writing from an executive summary down to implementation details), and produce long-form output (the SKILL.md says 10 to 100+ pages) with ten suggested sections from Executive Summary to Appendices. One line is worth the install: explain design rationale only when supported by code comments, decision records, issues or confirmed maintainer context, label inferred rationale and unknown history, and never invent benchmarks or deployment and security details.

**Who it is for.** Teams that need an architecture manual or onboarding document generated from an existing codebase.

**Pros**
- The "label inferred rationale" rule is a rare honesty guard in a docs skill.
- Output is Markdown with `file_path:line_number` references to code.

**Cons**
- The weakest SKILL.md in this list. The boilerplate adds no information, and it is catalog-category Writing, not Coding.
- A 10 to 100+ page target is a lot of tokens for a single run.
- 2 installs and 10 views. Treat it as unproven.
- Nothing about keeping docs current as the code changes.

**Benefits for you.** A starting point for architecture docs, with an explicit warning against fabricated history.

**Pricing and limits.** Free, MIT.

**Skip it if** you want API reference docs or a changelog. For release notes, the catalog's [Docs Changelog](https://agentskills.codes/skills/docs-changelog) is narrower but fits Gemini CLI-style changelog work better; we did not rank it because it is written for one project's release process.

