## Head-to-head: the closest pairs

### Code Reviewer vs Code Review Excellence

**Pick Code Reviewer** if you want a 3,183-character checklist that outputs Critical, Improvements and Nitpicks, and you are fine telling the agent to run your own checks instead of `npm run preflight`. **Pick Code Review Excellence** if you review other people's work and care about tone, disagreements and time-boxing, and you will install it with `degit` so the two reference files come along. They overlap, so install one per project, not both. If you want the least context spent, Code Reviewer wins; if you want the most review coaching, Code Review Excellence does.

### Git Commit vs Git Advanced Workflows vs Resolve Conflicts

These are three different jobs. Git Commit makes a clean commit and, by its Safety Rules, will not push or skip hooks unless you tell it to. Git Advanced Workflows is a recipe card for rebasing, bisecting and recovering, with good habits but no hard prohibitions. Resolve Conflicts is the one to reach for when a merge has already gone wrong, because it stops and shows you a plan first. If you can only install one, install Git Commit: it prevents the most common mistake (committing too much) and costs 8,575 characters when invoked. Add Resolve Conflicts on repos with long-lived branches.

### Refactor vs Software Architecture

Refactor is a process skill: tests first, small steps, commit often. Software Architecture is a style skill: early returns, no `utils` files, search for a library before writing one. They conflict in one place worth knowing. Refactor says functions should stay under 50 lines; Software Architecture says to decompose above 80 and also states 50 as a goal. If you install both, tell the agent which limit your team uses. Software Architecture has the higher install count (333 against 8), but Refactor's "tests first" rule is the more valuable constraint in our reading.

## Skills and MCP servers that work with these picks

Every pick above links to its catalog page. Three more catalog entries sit just outside the 15 and are worth knowing about, each confirmed to return 200 on November 25, 2026:

- [MCP Builder](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-builder) (anthropics/skills, 136 installs, 215 views): a guide for building MCP servers, with scripts and a reference folder. We left it out of the 15 because it is for people building integrations, not for everyday coding. It pairs naturally with Claude Automation Recommender: the recommender suggests MCP servers, and MCP Builder helps when none exists for your service.
- [Skill Creator](https://agentskills.codes/skills/skill-creator) (anthropics/skills, 128 installs, 200 views): Anthropic's skill for writing new skills. Its SKILL.md is 32,987 characters with agents, assets, an eval viewer, references and scripts, so it is the heaviest thing we looked at. Use it when you want to turn your own team's conventions into a skill rather than install someone else's.
- [Docs Changelog](https://agentskills.codes/skills/docs-changelog) (google-gemini/gemini-cli, 4 installs, 27 views): a step-by-step procedure for generating changelog files from GitHub release information. It is written for the Gemini CLI project, so treat it as a template.

On the MCP side, Boris Cherny (@bcherny) posted on November 24, 2026 that MCP servers can now ship with their own skills, so Claude Code knows how to use them out of the box. That is a first-party sign that skills and MCP servers are meant to be paired. Anthropic also has a video on writing your own skill:

