## Best for X: decision table

| If you want... | Install | Why |
|---|---|---|
| The agent to verify a web UI | Webapp Testing | Server lifecycle helper plus Playwright patterns |
| Clean conventional commits | Git Commit | Reads repo rules, does not push unless asked |
| A safe merge-conflict workflow | Resolve Conflicts | Plan, approval, then edits |
| A quick review of a PR or diff | Code Reviewer | 3,183 characters, severity buckets |
| Review coaching and tone | Code Review Excellence | Time-boxed phases, disagreement handling |
| Correct Vitest syntax | Vitest | Version-stamped, 19 reference files |
| Better pytest tests | Python Testing Patterns | AAA structure, markers, coverage gates |
| A repeatable debugging loop | Debugging Strategies | Reproduce first, one change at a time |
| Safer history rewrites | Git Advanced Workflows | `--force-with-lease`, backup branch |
| A staged major upgrade | Dependency Upgrade | One major at a time with a rollback plan |
| Refactors that keep behavior | Refactor | "Without tests, you're not refactoring" |
| A consistent code style | Software Architecture | Naming and library-first rules |
| Architecture docs from code | Docs Architect | Honest about inferred rationale |
| Setting up Claude Code on a repo | Claude Automation Recommender | Read-only recommendations |
| Long plans with review gates | Subagent Driven Development | Spec review, then quality review |

## Common mistakes

1. **Installing all 15.** Invoked skill bodies add up, and Claude Code carries over only the first 5,000 tokens of each invoked skill after compaction, within a shared 25,000-token budget (per its docs). Install the four or five you will use.
2. **Trusting the hosted zip for skills with references.** Nine of our 15 depend on files the zip leaves out.
3. **Ignoring hardcoded paths and commands.** Resolve Conflicts and Code Reviewer both assume a specific project layout.
4. **Reading install counts as quality.** The most-installed Coding skill had 1,555 installs; the median of ours is well under 100.
5. **Stacking overlapping skills.** Code Reviewer plus Code Review Excellence plus the bundled `/code-review` can give conflicting output formats.
6. **Skipping the read.** A SKILL.md is instructions your agent will follow with your credentials and file access. Read it before installing, especially any that run scripts.

## Setup checklist

- Pick a scope: personal (`~/.claude/skills/`) for habits you want everywhere, project (`.claude/skills/`) for team conventions in version control.
- Use `degit` for any skill whose catalog page or section above says it has scripts or references.
- Read the SKILL.md and any bundled script before the first run.
- Fix hardcoded paths and commands (`.forge/...`, `npm run preflight`) to match your repo.
- Invoke the skill by its slash command to confirm it loads, and add one or two at a time.

## How we evaluated

Here is exactly what we did on November 25, 2026.

**What we did.**
- Queried the agentskills.codes API (`/api/v1/skills`) for the terms coding, testing, debugging, git, refactor and docs, sorted by installs, and read the Coding category pages. The catalog reported 11,524 skills in total and 6,343 in Coding.
- For each of the 15 picks, fetched the catalog's markdown page and the SKILL.md from the GitHub source, and read the GitHub file end to end or by section. We compared the two copies after whitespace normalization.
- Asked the GitHub API for each repo's star count, license and archive status, and for the last commit that touched each skill's own folder.
- Downloaded all 15 hosted zips and listed their contents (one file each).
- Ran `npx degit` against all 15 source folders and counted the files (all exited 0).
- Confirmed every link in this post returns HTTP 200 (48 URLs checked), verified the YouTube video through oEmbed and the X posts through the X syndication endpoint, and read each client's skills documentation for the works-with matrix.

**What we did not do.** We did not run these skills against a real repository, measure token usage, compare output quality or time any workflow. So we do not claim any skill "improves" results, and the pros and cons describe what the instructions say and what is missing from them, not measured outcomes. The bundled Claude Code skills (`/code-review`, `/debug` and others) were not run either, so we make no claim about whether a third-party skill beats them. For that reason the title says "Reviewed", not "Tested". We also did not sample Reddit or other forums; the ranking is ours.

**Source of facts.** Installs and views come from the catalog API; stars, licenses and commit dates from the GitHub API; skill behavior from the GitHub SKILL.md; client paths from each vendor's documentation. Installs and stars change daily, so treat them as a November 25, 2026 snapshot.

## FAQ

### What are the best Claude Code skills for developers?
Our top picks from the agentskills.codes catalog are Webapp Testing for UI verification, Git Commit for safe commits, Resolve Conflicts for merge work, Code Reviewer for fast reviews, and Vitest or Python Testing Patterns for unit tests. The full ranking of 15 is above, with the catches for each.

### Where do I put a skill so Claude Code finds it?
In a folder with a `SKILL.md`. Claude Code reads personal skills from `~/.claude/skills/<name>/` and project skills from `.claude/skills/<name>/`. The folder name, or the frontmatter `name` if you set one, becomes the slash command. If the same name exists in two places, enterprise wins over personal and personal wins over project (per the Claude Code docs).

### Is the hosted zip the same as the GitHub skill?
The SKILL.md body is the same (we compared all 15 after normalizing whitespace). The zip contains only that file, though. Nine of the 15 skills have scripts, references or templates in their GitHub folder that the zip omits. Use `npx degit` for those.

### How do the skills load, and do they cost tokens all the time?
Per the Claude Code docs, skill descriptions sit in context so Claude knows what exists, and the full skill content loads only when invoked. So an installed skill costs a little all the time and a lot when used. The heaviest we read, Resolve Conflicts, is 17,965 characters.

### Do these skills work outside Claude Code?
The format is shared. Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI, OpenCode and Hermes each document the folder-with-SKILL.md layout, and Cursor and OpenCode also read `.claude/skills`. Some skills, like Subagent Driven Development, assume Claude Code features such as subagents and TodoWrite. We did not run these skills in the other clients.

### What is a Claude skills library?
A catalog of reusable skill folders you can install. The agentskills.codes catalog lists 11,524 skills as of November 25, 2026, and each page shows the source repo, a hosted download and the install command. [Anthropic's own repository](https://github.com/anthropics/skills) is another source.

### What does Reddit recommend?
We did not read Reddit or any forum for this post, so we cannot say. Our ranking comes from reading the SKILL.md files and checking their repos. If you see a skill recommended on Reddit, apply the same check: open the SKILL.md, look at what it tells the agent to do, and check when its folder last changed.

### Are skills safe to install?
A skill is instructions plus optional scripts that your agent follows with your file and shell access. Read the SKILL.md and any script first. Of our 15, only Webapp Testing and Resolve Conflicts bundle executable scripts, and Claude Automation Recommender is explicitly read-only. We did not audit the scripts line by line for security.

### Which skills should I avoid?
Not avoid, but be cautious: Docs Architect has 2 installs and a boilerplate-heavy SKILL.md, Debugging Strategies has 3 installs, and Software Architecture auto-loads on almost any coding task. Skip any skill whose rules contradict your team's `CLAUDE.md`.

### How often are these skills updated?
It varies. Git Commit's folder changed on November 5, 2026 and Vitest's on October 23, 2026, while Resolve Conflicts' last folder commit was November 27, 2025 and Subagent Driven Development's December 20, 2025. Old is not automatically bad, but check the date and read the file.

### Can I write my own skill instead?
Yes. Anthropic's Skill Creator, listed in the catalog, is a skill for that. A skill is a folder with a SKILL.md whose frontmatter has a name and description, so a short one is easy to write. Put your team's conventions into one and commit it to `.claude/skills/`.

